    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a major issue with my dark chocolate batches. i'm seeing this weird white streaking/dusty look appearing on the surface of the bars after they set. it's like a bloom. can you explain why this is happening from a chemistry standpoint and how i can fix my tempering process to stop it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The white streaking you're seeing is likely sugar bloom or fat bloom, which is a common issue when dealing with chocolate. If it's sugar bloom, it's caused by moisture in the air dissolving some of the surface sugar, which then recrystallizes as the moisture evaporates. To fix this, you need to ensure your environment is dry, and you should also ensure your chocolate is perfectly tempered. A proper tempering process stabilizes the cocoa butter crystals, making the chocolate less susceptible to these surface issues.",
